WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.280
Every cell in the cluster now has proneural factor.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.120
So what picks one?

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.360
The cells signal to each other through Notch.

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.520
The signal says, not you.

5
00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.410
Delta is a protein in the membrane of one cell.

6
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.510
It binds Notch on the cell it touches, and pulls.

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.900
Two cuts then free the inner part of Notch.

8
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.220
That part enters the nucleus.

9
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:32.050
There, with a DNA-binding protein called CSL, it turns on target genes.

10
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:37.663
In a proneural cluster, the targets that matter are the Enhancer of split, genes.

11
00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:41.493
They make repressors that switch proneural genes off.

12
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:44.973
Proneural factors do two things in return.

13
00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:47.093
They raise their own expression.

14
00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:50.563
And, in the standard model, they raise Delta.

15
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:54.973
Now follow two neighbours, A and B, one step at a time.

16
00:00:55.916 --> 00:00:58.856
Step one. A has a little more Delta.

17
00:00:59.856 --> 00:01:02.736
B gets more Notch activity, so B makes more repressor.

18
00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:06.546
Step two. B is repressed.

19
00:01:07.386 --> 00:01:10.006
It has less proneural factor, so it makes less Delta.

20
00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:14.646
Step three. The signal coming back to A is weaker.

21
00:01:15.526 --> 00:01:17.886
A makes less repressor, and more proneural factor.

22
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:20.286
So A makes more Delta.

23
00:01:21.126 --> 00:01:25.766
The returning signal pushes A's Delta up again, in the direction of its original

24
00:01:25.766 --> 00:01:29.946
lead. Whether the lead ends up bigger depends on how steep the loop is.

25
00:01:30.726 --> 00:01:31.746
That comes next.

26
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.500
Each cell inhibits its neighbour.

27
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.400
So A inhibits the cell that inhibits A.

28
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.340
Two inhibitions in a row act as positive feedback.

29
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.740
This is a switch-making arrangement.

30
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:49.693
If the loop is steep enough, noise gives one cell a lead, and the loop magnifies it.

31
00:01:50.473 --> 00:01:52.833
In this ideal pair, which cell wins is chance.

32
00:01:53.866 --> 00:01:56.986
Predict. Neither cell has any Notch.

33
00:01:57.766 --> 00:01:58.806
What happens to the pair?

34
00:02:05.216 --> 00:02:07.336
Neither cell can be told, not you.

35
00:02:08.116 --> 00:02:10.936
Both keep proneural genes on, and both become neural.

36
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.370
Too many neural cells.

37
00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.890
Fly geneticists call this the neurogenic phenotype, here in miniature.

38
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:24.250
Now the reverse. Switch Notch on in both cells, whatever Delta they receive.

39
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:27.093
Repressor stays high in both.

40
00:02:28.053 --> 00:02:30.893
Neither becomes neural, and the cluster gives no precursor.

41
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:35.690
A double negative between neighbours acts as positive feedback.

42
00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:37.690
A small lead can grow.
